 "note": "The reference shape, and the one the parity test's equality assertion runs on. Every rule the two sides must agree on is exercised here at least once, ON A ROW THAT IS ACTUALLY REPORTED — three rules were placed on entities the derivation dropped earlier and were pinned by nothing until mutation testing said so: a stored label on a device that was never offline, a `disabled` mapping that the config-debris rule already removed, and `isUnavailable(absent)`, which is only reachable through a mesh-derived id Home Assistant has never heard of.\n\nD12 (2026-08-22): tkt-3..6 are the fault rows on which `status` and `resolvedAt` tell different stories. They exist so the fault-parity test can tell the two rules apart; a corpus of well-formed tickets passes against either and proves nothing.\n\nCategories (2026-09-04): switch.garden_lamp, switch.gate_release, switch.wall_outlet, binary_sensor.patio_door, sensor.hall_temperature, sensor.router_status, fan.study, camera.drive (a stored LEGACY default, which must be ignored) and light.porch (a stored owner choice, which must win) each reach one rule of `effectiveCategory` on a SELECTABLE id, so the add-on's twin in `adapters/categories.py` is pinned by a reported row and not by a corpus that any twin would match.",
